# The Dual Trace: Resonant Motion, Resonant Affect, and the Mechanism of Antichesis in Aristotle's Ontology of Motion and Time 2

## 1. Aristotle on Motion: Kinesis as the Being of Becoming

Aristotle's treatment of motion in the *Physics* constitutes one of the most enduring philosophical efforts to articulate becoming as a genuine mode of being rather than as a mere deficiency or negation of stable substance. Indeed, for Aristotle, motion (κίνησις) is not something that stands apart from beings; it is, as Heidegger emphasizes in his careful reading of *Physics* Gamma 1, the "how of the being of beings," a determination that belongs to entities insofar as they exist in potentiality and actuality (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 262–263). Heidegger's gloss on this passage underscores that motion is not παρὰ τὰ πράγματα—not "alongside" or external to things—but is rather inherent in the way beings disclose themselves between δύναμις and ἐντελέχεια (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 262–263). Thus the very ontological grammar of Aristotle's physics resists any effort to reduce motion to a merely spatial displacement; motion is, in the most fundamental sense, the unfolding of what a thing can be into what it is, the passage from potentiality to actuality that defines the natural world.

We Aristotle unfolds this understanding through his analysis of the infinite and the continuous. In *Physics* Book III, (Hawhee 2011)[10] "the infinite has this mode of existence: one thing is always being taken after another, and each thing that is taken is always finite, but always different" (Aristotle, *Physics*, pp. 39–40)[1]. Here the infinite is not a substance or a "this" (τόδε τι) but something whose being consists in an ongoing, never-completed process of successive differentiation; it exists in the way "the day or the games" exist, as a serial unfolding rather than as a discrete entity (Aristotle, *Physics*, pp. 39–40). This characterization is crucial for understanding motion, because it reveals that kinesis partakes of the same ontological modality as the infinite: it is a process that is always underway, always taking something after something else, never arriving at a static completion that would annul its character as motion. Similarly, Aristotle insists that change "is always faster or slower, whereas time is not; for fast and slow are defined by time—fast is what moves much in a short time, slow what moves little in a long time; but time is not defined by time, by being either a certain amount or a certain kind of it" (Aristotle, *Physics*, p. 59)[2]. The distinction between change and time at this juncture serves to protect the autonomy of motion as a phenomenon: change has an intrinsic variability—faster and slower—that time, as its measure, does not possess.

Hence the Aristotelian account of motion opens onto a broader ecological and rhetorical significance. James Gibson, in his account of ambient optic structure, similarly foregrounds the environment as a field of ongoing differentiation rather than a static container; when (Hawhee 2011)[11] "substances also differ in their readiness to evaporate or sublimate, and this too affects the persistence of their surfaces," he identifies a material flux in the environment that resonates with (Aristotle 2014)[12] motion is a constitutive feature of the natural world (Gibson, *The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception*, pp. 114–116). Specifically, both thinkers treat the world not as a set of fixed objects upon which motion is imposed, but as an arena in which change, persistence, and differentiation are co-originary phenomena. Kenneth Burke extends this insight into the domain of motive and rhetoric by arguing that a "reduction to a simplicity" is always a "reduction to a summarizing title or 'God term,'" beneath which we must ask "what complexities are subsumed" (Burke, *A Grammar of Motives*, pp. 126–127)[3]. Accordingly, any simplification of Aristotle's kinesis into mere physical locomotion would obscure the rich strata of qualitative, quantitative, and substantial change that Aristotle identifies as species of motion—a plurality that Burke's dramatistic method would insist we preserve.

## 2. Aristotle on Affect: Pathos, Comportment, and Resonant Disposition

If motion describes the ontological structure of becoming in Aristotle's natural philosophy, then affect (πάθος) describes the analogous structure within the ensouled being—specifically, the way in which the soul is moved by and toward the world. The relationship between motion and affect is not merely metaphorical; for Aristotle, phantasia is itself a kind of motion in the soul, a residual kinesis generated by the activity of perception that persists even after the external object has been removed. The image-making faculty thus cannot function independently of aisthesis; it is grounded in the prior operation of sense-perception, which is itself a mode of being-affected by the sensible world. Thus motion and affect share a common grammar: both involve a transition from potentiality to actuality, and both are irreducible to the static categories of substance.

Indeed, Heidegger's reading of Aristotle's πάθη as Befindlichkeiten—as modes of finding oneself disposed in the world, rather than as static pieces of knowledge—captures the dynamic character of Aristotelian affect with remarkable precision. In *Being and Time*, Heidegger develops this insight into an existential analytic in which time itself becomes the horizon of affective disclosure; (Hawhee 2011)[13] "this time which is 'universally' accessible in clocks is something that we come across as a present-at-hand multiplicity of 'nows,' so to speak, though the measuring of time is not directed thematically towards time as such" (Heidegger, *Being and Time*, pp. 470–471)[4]. The significance of this passage for our purposes is that it reveals how the ordinary understanding of time—as a succession of discrete "nows"—conceals the deeper temporal structure of affective comportment. When Aristotle's subject is moved by anger, pity, or fear, the experience is not a punctual event located at a single now-point; it is a resonant process that unfolds across time, drawing together memory, anticipation, and present sensation in a complex temporal weave. Similarly, Thomas Rickert observes that rhetorical responsiveness involves an ongoing attunement, a getting "in sync" with the kairotic moment, such that affective disposition is not separable from the temporal and environmental conditions of its emergence (Rickert, *Ambient Rhetoric*, p. 309).

Von Uexküll's concept of the Umwelt provides a further dimension for understanding affect as environmentally constituted. In his account of the perceptual world of organisms, von Uexküll describes how sensory qualities become perceptual marks that structure the organism's environment: "The sensation 'blue' becomes the 'blueness' of the sky, the sensation 'green' becomes the 'greenness' of the lawn, and so forth. We recognize the sky by the feature 'blue' and the lawn by the feature 'green'" (von Uexkull, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 52–53). This transposition of sensation into environmental feature-marks parallels the Aristotelian process whereby the soul's affective capacity transforms the raw data of perception into meaningful, action-guiding dispositions. Subsequently, in both frameworks, the organism does not passively receive sensory data but actively constitutes its environment through the marks that its perceptual and affective organs impress upon objects—what von Uexküll calls the "effect mark" or Wirkmal (von Uexkull, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 52–53). Burke's observation that a dialectical account must "divide response into both physiological and mental moments, while at the same time admonishing that such dualism must be discounted as a mere convenience of discourse" reinforces this point, since Aristotle's affect is precisely such a tertium quid—a phenomenon that is neither purely bodily nor purely intellectual, but rather the resonant interplay of both (Burke, *A Grammar of Motives*, pp. 261–262).

## 3. Aristotle on Ontology: Substance, Contrariety, and the Mechanism of Antichesis

The mechanism we may call antichesis—the structured opposition through which being articulates itself into contraries and thereby generates the space for motion and change—lies at the heart of Aristotle's ontology. Aristotle makes clear in the *Metaphysics* that the primary being, the unmoved mover, "is not movable either in itself or accidentally, but produces the primary eternal and single movement," and he insists that "the mover is eternal and prior to the moved, and that which is prior to a substance must be a substance" (Aristotle, *Metaphysics*, pp. 146–147)[5]. Hence the ontological hierarchy is secured by a principle that is itself exempt from motion yet serves as the ground of all motion; this is the architectonic structure of antichesis, whereby the unmoved and the moved, the eternal and the temporal, are held in structured opposition. The very possibility of change depends upon this opposition, for without the stable reference point of the unmoved mover, the infinite regression of causes would render motion unintelligible.

Specifically, Aristotle's treatment of contradictory predication in *Metaphysics* Book logical dimension of antichesis. (Hawhee 2011)[14] "if there is movement, and something moved, and everything is moved out of something and into something, it follows that that which is moved must first be in that out of which it is to be moved, and then not be in it, and move into the other and come to be in it, and that the contradictory statements are not true at the same time" (Aristotle, *Metaphysics*, pp. 130–131)[6]. This passage reveals that motion presupposes a principle of non-contradiction operating at the ontological level: a thing cannot simultaneously be in the state from which it departs and the state into which it arrives. Thus antichesis is not merely a logical principle but an ontological condition of becoming; without the real distinction between contraries, there would be no passage from one to the other. The Aristotelian ontology therefore resists both the Heraclitean thesis that contradictories can be simultaneously true and the Parmenidean thesis that change is impossible; it navigates between these extremes by establishing contrariety as the structural ground of motion.

Burke's concept of substance provides a useful rhetorical parallel to this ontological operation. (Hawhee 2011)[15], "one may say 'it is substantially true' precisely at a time when on the basis of the evidence, it would be much more accurate to say, 'it is not true'" (Burke, *A Grammar of Motives*, pp. 73–74)[7]. Substance, in Burke's analysis, is thus always haunted by its own negation; it contains within itself the dialectical tension between affirmation and denial that mirrors Aristotle's account of contrariety. Accordingly, Fodor's insistence that reductivism guarantees only "a weak version of physicalism" that "is compatible with a non-physicalist ontology since it is compatible with the possibility that x's satisfying S should not itself be a physical event" registers a parallel concern with the irreducibility of ontological levels (Fodor, *Special Sciences*, pp. 3–4). The mechanism of antichesis, then, operates not only within Aristotle's framework but resonates with modern philosophical debates about the relationship between different levels of description and the irreducibility of emergent properties.

## 4. Aristotle on Time: The Numbered Now and Resonant Duration

Time, in Aristotle's account, is the number of motion with respect to before and after; it is thus neither identical with motion nor entirely separable from it. As we have already seen, Aristotle distinguishes time from change by noting that change is "always faster or slower, whereas time is not; for fast and slow are defined by time" (Aristotle, *Physics*, p. 59)[8]. This asymmetry between time and change reveals that time functions as a measure or standard against which the variability of motion is calibrated; yet time itself only becomes manifest through motion, specifically through the determinate celestial motions that mark out countable units. The relationship is thus one of mutual dependence rather than simple identity: motion requires time as its measure, and time requires motion as its manifestation.

The question of temporal continuity is equally central. Aristotle insists in *Physics* Book VIII that "all that matters is the intervention of a time: it is this that prevents the change from being continuous; so, too, in our previous instances the important thing was not the relation of contrariety but the impossibility of the two processes being present at the same time" (Aristotle, *Physics*, p. 125)[9]. Here the "intervention of a time" between successive changes is what distinguishes genuinely continuous motion from a mere sequence of discrete states; it is the temporal gap between contrary processes—not their contrariety per se—that structures the rhythm of becoming. This finding reinforces the mechanism of antichesis discussed in the previous section, since the real opposition of contraries requires temporal separation for its actualization. Heidegger's observation that ordinary clock-time presents itself as "a present-at-hand multiplicity of 'nows'" reveals the derivative character of this everyday understanding; the succession of discrete now-points conceals the deeper structure of time as the horizon within which motion unfolds as a continuous, resonant process (Heidegger, *Being and Time*, pp. 470–471).

Gibson's ecological approach offers a complementary perspective on the temporality of perception and environment. When Gibson notes that substances differ in their readiness to persist or change—to "evaporate or sublimate"—he identifies a temporal dimension of the material environment that is inseparable from its spatial structure (Gibson, *The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception*, pp. 114–116). The affordances of the environment are thus not static properties but temporally inflected possibilities: a surface of support affords resting only so long as it persists, and its persistence is a function of material time. Similarly, von Uexküll's account of the functional cycle—whereby the organism's perception cells "transpose a perception mark into the environment"—implies a temporal loop in which the organism and its world are bound together in an ongoing, rhythmic exchange of marks and counter-marks (von Uexkull, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 121–124). The dual trace of the title thus emerges here in its fullest articulation: motion leaves its trace in the temporal structure of the world, and affect leaves its trace in the perceptual and environmental marks through which the ensouled being constitutes its dwelling. Time, accordingly, is not a neutral container but the very medium through which the resonances of motion and affect are registered, measured, and preserved. Burke's rhetorical insight that the many "god-terms" dotting human thought constitute a "profoundly genuine" rhetoric reminds us that our conceptual attempts to name time, motion, and affect are themselves acts of constitutive naming, imprinting upon the flux of becoming the marks by which we render it intelligible (Burke, *A Rhetoric of Motives*, pp. 314–315). The Aristotelian ontology of motion and time is, in this sense, always already a rhetoric—a structured act of disclosure that attunes the user to the resonant, dual-traced character of being in becoming.

